{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/1564562-c20-mist-effects-on-long-term-cantrips","id":1564562,"title":"[C20] Mist Effects on Long-Term Cantrips","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Changeling: The Dreaming"],"posts":[{"date":"2026-10-06T07:52:17-07:00","id":1564562,"title":"[C20] Mist Effects on Long-Term Cantrips","content":"I've been reading Changeling 20th Anniversary Edition, and one thing that isn't entirely clear to me is what the effect of the mists are on long-term cantrips. An example provided in the book is in the section &quot;Seven Dreams Brought to Life,&quot; under &quot;The Pig and the Selkie,&quot; page 236. In the example, the Selkie performs a cantrip to transform a creep from a man into a woman, with the duration lasting a whole month.<br /> <br /> The question that comes to me is; how does the Mist cloud the memory? Does the man turned woman go through the entire month fully aware of what had happened, with their memory beginning to cloud at the end of the effect? Or do they experience the effects of the Mist immediately afterwards \u2014 which brings even more questions to my mind? Do they just begin to forget <i>how</i> they became a woman, and realize they have suddenly changed sex, or do they begin to forget the times they <i>weren't</i> a woman?<br /> <br /> A follow-up is how the Mists interact with witnesses to long-term cantrips. One example (and the only other post I've seen asking this same question) is by Reddit user Vulcan7200, who gave the, albeit extreme example, of using Winter 5 Scene 5 to put an entire college campus into Stasis.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If a Changeling froze a college campus for a week, including everyone currently within the building how would the Mists cover it up? Would it start covering it up immediately, trying to make people not even notice the campus while it's frozen? Would it only start covering it up once the effect ends, essentially causing panic for that week that everyone eventually forgets about? </div> </div> </div>I couldn't seem to find anything in C20, but if there is an older sourcebook that discusses the effect of long-term cantrips, I would appreciate being pointed in the right direction, thank you <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28584-matter911","name":"Matter911","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":1,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-10-06T10:12:22-07:00","id":1564566,"title":"","content":"The first thing is to understand one important aspect of the Mists: it conceals the Dreaming, not magic. Not everything you do is forgotten, they're not an excuse for recklessness.<br /> <br /> Any effect in Changeling is classified as either Wyrd or Chimeric. This tells you everything you need to know and is really simple:<br /> <br /> Chimeric doesn't affect people who can't see Glamour in the moment, and those who see are affected by the Mists.<br /> <br /> Wyrd affects anyone, but they're not affected by the Mists.<br /> <br /> Basically that. You can fuzzy the lines when it's interesting, but then it's up to you as ST.<br /> <br /> One important aspect of the game is the role and meaning of the Dreaming. It is like a real intelligence that interferes in relevant effects with intention and a serious grasp of tropes. Or, in other words, a request for the ST to step in and deliberately make arbitrary decisions.<br /> <br /> So don't feel bad about your decisions in Changeling. They're literally the nature of the realm where the Fae take their magic from.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}